A||||14||Notchit||Curious!||||4||
Z||000000||Notchit||01-12-2001||12:39 PM||||Alright, 2 days ago me and a friend were driving in her 1982 Buick LeSabre. we heard a rattling and then there was loss of power so we pulled over. then BAM the radiator hose blew up, it ripped the hood material (on top) to shreds and had enough force to wreck the cruse control thing. We went for help to a farmers house, and called a friend to pick us up. when he showed up we went to a store to ge the rad hose (by the way this was during a spare, and I missed most of school  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ) and some more anti freeze. we hooked all of it up, filled the rad, then went to start the car. siezed. i have never seen a seized engine....quite sad. so now its towed to a mechanic. we found out the thermostat went. my question is this: IF the thermostat went in my Fiero, would i be warned ahead of time, and also if my anti freeze leaked out of the car while i was driving, would there be a warning for that before it was too late. <P>thanks very much<BR><P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Outside_side2(sml2).jpg"> <BR>_________________________||||reg||4
Z||000001||My7Fieros||01-12-2001||01:12 PM||||Oh yeah you'll know. The temp guage will let you know..I suspect that that Buick didnt have a guage, only an idiot light (I hate those things)..by the time they light up, you're usually screwed!<BR>NEVER drive your car if the temp gets into the red....itll ruin it for sure!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GeeDee86GT||01-12-2001||01:26 PM||||You can also buy a thermostat which is guaranteed to stay open in case of failure, which will at least prevent cooking the engine.||||reg||14
Z||000003||terryk||01-12-2001||02:50 PM||||Once you've heard the sounds of detonation from overheating, you rarely forget it!||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||01-12-2001||06:28 PM||||That engine was like this inside  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FIRE6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||MoneypitGT||01-12-2001||10:46 PM||||Yeah you'll know about 2 mintues after it happens. I pulled my fiero out of the garage one day and let it run to warm up...it warmed up alright, overheated in only a few mintues, needle was buried. Checked the thermostat, stuck closed. Pulled it out, changed the oil and was good as new.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-13-2001||02:50 AM||||I had the same experience as MonkeyPit,  the thermostat stuck open about 3 miles from home.  I drove home in the red (less than 5 minutes total).<BR>My thermostat was stuck somewhat open, so the engine somehow survived.  Everybody says the 2.5L Duke 4 cylinder is fragile, but I have to say I was impressed that there seems to be no harm done.  There was no warning, the needle just shot up suddenly.  I thought the sensor had failed since I had washed the engine the day before.<P>WARNING: Never drive your Fiero when the temperature is in the red.<P>The incident makes me worry about the potential for other surprises.  I am  planning to rebuild the engine with a basic porting job, high flow oil pump, and metal timing gears.  Actually, my car has 91K miles on it and I really worry about that phenolic plastic timing gear going on me.  I'd rather just fix it now.  <P>The trouble is that once you take the head off of the engine, it's really hard to know where to stop rebuilding.  That's why I am going to just do it all at once.<P>First, I have to pay off the IRS in April.  Those bastards always get their share don't they????<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Notchit||01-13-2001||06:34 PM||||What kind of thermostat do I need thats guaranteed to stay open in case of failure? like model and whatnot, cause now thats what i'm afraid of now, the engine siezing....it was the worst site...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Outside_side2(sml2).jpg"> <BR>_________________________||||reg||4
Z||000008||Cooter||01-13-2001||06:51 PM||||I saw those thermo's a while back on Shade Tree Mechanic (yes, it has been a while). I believe they were called Fail Safe||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero Fanatic||01-14-2001||04:53 AM||||I don't worry about my thermostat, Because I took it out completely. Cooler is better than hotter.||||reg||6
Z||000010||rogergarrison||01-14-2001||08:28 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero Fanatic:<BR><B>I don't worry about my thermostat, Because I took it out completely. Cooler is better than hotter.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That used to be OK. but engines now wont cirulate properly with no thermostat. Id suggest u put one back in. I use a 160* myself.<P>Also, most of todays engines are alloy/alum, and theyll fry in seconds. watch gage n pull over immeadiatly now, even turn it off and coast may save you thousands.<P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||01-14-2001||08:44 AM||||I've run both my cars very very close to the red when I've been stuck in traffic before.. the '84 because the cooling system needs a major overhaul, and the fan doesn't work; the GT mainly because the fan didn't work, either.  Also had my GT freeze solid a few times because I had too much water, had to drive home from my g/f's house like that once.  ~15 mile drive, sitting right next to redline for about the last half, and getting some steam from the engine compartment.  I thought I might have burst something, but after draining a bunch of coolant and replacing it with straight anti-freeze it's been running perfect.  The 84's survived, too.  After 12-14 years hoses and head gaskets tend to get pretty well bonded to the engine..||||reg||
Z||000012||Notchit||01-14-2001||07:45 PM||||Ohh yeah that reminds me, when i start the car, and let it run for maybe 3-5 mins, the engine gets pretty hot, not the top part that says Fiero on it, but lower down....the temp gauge reads that its totaly cool....is this normal?||||reg||4
Z||000013||Notchit||01-14-2001||07:45 PM||||Ohh yeah that reminds me, when i start the car, and let it run for maybe 3-5 mins, the engine gets pretty hot, not the top part that says Fiero on it, but lower down....the temp gauge reads that its totaly cool....is this normal?<P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Outside_side2(sml2).jpg"> <BR>_________________________||||reg||4
Z||000014||Larry Moss||01-15-2001||04:55 PM||||Hi:<P>It is likely that the car's cooling system was actually froze.  Because the coolant in the system does not freely move throughout, most of it will remain frozen, and the other part will boil over.  Once it is too pressurized, it explodes through a hose, or head gasket, or both.<P>Do not look at it as a problem.  Just make sure you have a fresh mixture of antifreeze and water.  That should provide protection to -37 degrees.<P>||||reg||1
